$html = <<<'HTML'
<p>The phrase circulates at conferences, in threads and in hallway chats and sounds like comfort for those who write: <em>"when all texts are the same, human-written ones will rise in value again"</em>. It has the shape of a reassuring prophecy: the machine floods the world with correct, gray text, and the authentic, being scarce, is worth more. It has some truth, and also several hidden assumptions. <strong>How true is it?</strong></p>
<p>Instead of answering "yes" or "no", this article does what is best to do with any resounding claim: <strong>break it into checkable parts</strong>, look for the best available evidence on each, say with what level of confidence and where what we do not know lies. It includes an <a href="/descargas/textos-iguales/evaluacion-afirmacion-textos-humanos.xlsx">Excel workbook</a> (in Spanish) with the breakdown (five parts, with my editable confidence levels) and a <a href="/descargas/textos-iguales/medidor_homogeneidad.py">Python script</a> that measures how alike the texts of a group are, with fictional example data. I verified the workbook in Microsoft Excel 16 and against an independent calculation; the script, against a second implementation written separately. Sources reviewed on <strong>October 10, 2026</strong>.</p>
<p class="notice"><strong>Warning.</strong> The workbook's confidence levels are <strong>my judgment</strong> on the cited evidence, not published data, and are there for you to change. The studies are specific (particular tasks, populations and platforms) and their results may not extrapolate. The script's example texts are fictional.</p>

<h2>The claim, broken down</h2>
{{img:partes}}
<p>For the phrase to be true, five things must hold <em>at once</em>: <strong>P1)</strong> AI texts will tend to look alike; <strong>P2)</strong> readers will not be able to tell them from human ones; <strong>P3)</strong> people will value more what they know is human; <strong>P4)</strong> that value will translate into higher prices; and <strong>P5)</strong> what the verb "will return" suggests will happen, that there is a precedent. A chain of assumptions is as strong as its weakest link. Let us look at each.</p>

<h2>P1. Do AI texts look alike?</h2>
<p><strong>Partly true (confidence 60%).</strong> In an experiment published in <em>Science Advances</em> in 2024, Anil Doshi and Oliver Hauser asked 293 non-professional writers to write an eight-sentence story, with or without ideas generated by a language model. Stories with AI ideas were rated <strong>more creative, better written and more enjoyable</strong>, especially among less creative writers, but <strong>they resembled one another more</strong> than those written unaided: individual gain, loss of collective diversity. The authors describe it as a social dilemma.</p>
<p>Another result points the same way from a different angle: in a <em>Nature</em> paper (2024), Ilia Shumailov and colleagues showed that models trained recursively on data generated by other models <strong>lose the "tails" of the distribution</strong> (the rare, the minority) and collapse toward more uniform outputs. That does not say all AI text is the same, but that, <em>if the web fills with synthetic text and models train on it</em>, diversity erodes.</p>
<p>The limits: the experiment used short texts by amateur writers and ideas without later editing; a text with specific instructions, its own style and human review can be very different. "All the same" is an exaggeration; <strong>"default texts tend to converge"</strong> is what is defensible. You can check it in your classroom with the script (see below).</p>

<h2>P2. Can readers not tell a human text from an AI one?</h2>
<p><strong>Probable (80%), with nuances.</strong> Maurice Jakesch, Jeffrey Hancock and Mor Naaman (<em>PNAS</em>, 2023) had people read self-presentations (like those of a dating profile or an employment platform), human and AI-generated: readers' accuracy was barely <strong>50 to 52%</strong>, that is, nearly chance. And it was not pure chance: many used the same cues (first-person pronouns, contractions, family topics), which turned out to be wrong, so AI could produce texts perceived as "more human than human". Likewise, automatic detectors are not a reliable solution (see <a href="/guia-uso-inteligencia-artificial-educacion-evidencia-normas-colombia-matriz-excel/">the guide to using AI in education</a>, in Spanish).</p>
<p>The limits: they are short texts of a particular genre; an expert reader, a long text or knowing the author changes the picture. But the useful conclusion is another: <strong>if the text no longer proves its origin, value moves to what does prove it</strong> (identity, track record, process, reputation, evidence).</p>

<h2>P3. Do people value more what they know is human?</h2>
<p><strong>Partly true (65%).</strong> There is evidence of a bias in favor of the human <em>when the origin is disclosed</em>. Bellaiche and colleagues (<em>Cognitive Research: Principles and Implications</em>, 2023) gave different participants the same works labeled "created by a person" or "created by AI": those labeled human received more positive ratings, and the authors identify narrative and perceived effort as part of the mechanism. In communication, Schilke and Reimann's study on "the transparency dilemma" (<em>Organizational Behavior and Human Decision Processes</em>, 2025) found that disclosing AI use reduces trust in the one who discloses it (I summarize from coverage of the work; I did not read the full paper).</p>
<p>The limits: it is a label-conditional bias, and it varies by type of text. For an instruction manual, a routine email or a meeting summary, almost nobody cares who wrote it; for a memoir, an opinion column, medical advice or a recommendation letter, a lot. It is a <strong>niche</strong> value, not a general one.</p>

<h2>P4. Does that value translate into higher prices?</h2>
<p><strong>Weakly supported (25%), and it is the decisive part.</strong> What is observed so far points the other way for everyday text. Xiang Hui, Oren Reshef and Luofeng Zhou (<em>Organization Science</em>, 2024) studied a large freelance work platform after ChatGPT appeared: freelancers in highly exposed occupations (such as writing) suffered <strong>reductions in employment and earnings</strong>, and they found no evidence that higher prior quality protected them; they even suggest the best were proportionally more affected. It is a short-term study on one platform, but it points in the opposite direction from "value rises" for text as a service.</p>
<p>A second study, by Jingyi Cui, Gabriel Dias and Justin Ye (2025 working paper, not peer-reviewed), shows the mechanism that does seem to work: when an AI cover-letter writing tool was introduced on an employment platform, the correlation between a letter's "quality" and the chance of being contacted <strong>fell 51%</strong>, and employers turned to signals that are hard to fabricate, such as <strong>prior work history</strong>. That is: when text is cheap, <strong>value moves to what is verifiable</strong>.</p>
<p>And there is a market response in the form of a signal: the Authors Guild in the United States launched in early 2025 a "Human Authored" mark for books, with a public verification database, and later extended it to all authors published there. It is an indication that human origin is beginning to be certified, not that more is paid for it: I found no price data.</p>

<h2>P5. "Will return"? The historical analogy</h2>
<p><strong>Partly true (40%), but it is analogy, not evidence.</strong> It is true that other industrialized goods (pottery, bread, furniture, recorded music) left a market of "handmade" objects or live performances at high prices. But that precedent looks more like a <strong>luxury niche</strong> than a general rise, and in that market most consumption moved to mass-produced goods. Applied to text, it suggests a two-speed outcome: generic text, cheapened to near zero; and a small segment where authorship, voice and accountability matter.</p>

<h2>The verdict: a narrower, more useful version</h2>
<p>With my confidences (editable in the workbook), the <strong>weighted average</strong> (the price part weighs double) is <strong>49%</strong>, that is, <strong>partly true</strong>; and if the five parts were independent, the <strong>joint probability</strong> that all hold would be barely <strong>3%</strong>. That 3% should not be taken literally (the parts are related and the figures are subjective), but it illustrates a rule: <strong>a claim that chains five assumptions is much less likely than each one separately</strong>. Of the five parts, one is "probable" (P2), three "partly true" and one "weakly supported" (P4). The mean evidence level is 2.4 out of 5.</p>
{{img:confianza}}
<pre><code>' Weighted mean confidence (confidence in E, weight in F)
=SUMPRODUCT(Partes!E4:E8,Partes!F4:F8)/SUM(Partes!F4:F8)             ' English
=SUMAPRODUCTO(Partes!E4:E8;Partes!F4:F8)/SUMA(Partes!F4:F8)         ' Spanish

' Joint probability if the parts were independent (in %)
=PRODUCT(Partes!E4:E8)/100^COUNT(Partes!E4:E8)*100                  ' English
=PRODUCTO(Partes!E4:E8)/100^CONTAR(Partes!E4:E8)*100                ' Spanish

' Reading according to the cut-offs in the Parametros sheet
=IF(B4>=Parametros!B3,"Probable",IF(B4>=Parametros!B4,"Parcialmente cierta","Poco respaldada"))   ' English
=SI(B4>=Parametros!B3;"Probable";SI(B4>=Parametros!B4;"Parcialmente cierta";"Poco respaldada"))   ' Spanish</code></pre>
<p>And the version that does hold up is more modest: <strong>when generic text is abundant, what will be valued is not "human text" but what is verifiably human, accountable and with its own voice or experience.</strong> That includes the signature that answers for what it says (journalism, expert opinion, professional advice), first-hand experience (memoir, testimony, original research), the judgment that selects and the relationship with a particular reader. Whoever writes filler, low-risk, voiceless text has reason to worry; whoever contributes something a machine cannot give (having been there, answering by name, knowing something not on the web) has reason to continue. One more nuance: the erosion of diversity described by model collapse may increase the value of human data <em>for those who train models</em> (licensing deals between AI companies and publishers have been announced; I did not verify their terms), which is not the same as value for the reader.</p>

<h2>Measure the homogeneity of your own texts</h2>
<p>The <a href="/descargas/textos-iguales/medidor_homogeneidad.py"><code>medidor_homogeneidad.py</code> script</a> (standard Python, nothing to install) calculates, for a group of texts, three indicators: <strong>mean similarity</strong> (average, over all pairs, of the Jaccard index of non-stop words), <strong>bigram diversity</strong> (distinct bigrams over total bigrams) and <strong>shared bigrams</strong> (those appearing in half or more of the texts). Without arguments, it runs with two <em>fictional</em> example groups of eight texts: one written with variety and one with the same template. With folders of .txt files, it measures yours.</p>
<pre><code>python medidor_homogeneidad.py                       # example with fictional data
python medidor_homogeneidad.py class_answers         # your texts (one .txt per student)

Grupo A (escritos con variedad, ficticio): 8 textos | similitud media 0.015 | diversidad de bigramas 0.976 | bigramas compartidos 1
Grupo B (misma plantilla, ficticio): 8 textos | similitud media 0.636 | diversidad de bigramas 0.292 | bigramas compartidos 29</code></pre>
{{img:script}}
<p>In the example, the eight templated texts share 64% of their words (on average, by pairs) and have 29 shared bigrams, against 1.5% and 1 for the varied texts. <strong>An honest reading:</strong> it is a simple measure, not an AI detector; a group with tightly closed instructions will have similar texts even if each person wrote theirs, and an AI text with a good prompt can be different. Use it to talk with your students about voice, variety and originality, not to accuse anyone. Do not paste texts with students' personal data into external tools.</p>

<h2>What it means for those who teach and learn to write</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>For teachers:</strong> if generic text no longer proves anything, assess the <strong>process</strong> (drafts, logs, conversation about what was written) and the <strong>voice</strong> (specificity, own experience, argued position), not just the product (see <a href="/guia-uso-inteligencia-artificial-educacion-evidencia-normas-colombia-matriz-excel/">the guide to using AI in education</a> and <a href="/actividades-con-ia-taxonomia-de-bloom-banco-36-actividades-prompts-planificador-excel/">AI activities by Bloom level</a>, both in Spanish).</li>
<li><strong>For students:</strong> what is worth training is what a machine cannot give in your place: looking closely, having something to say, truly citing and answering by name. Writing well remains a way of thinking well.</li>
<li><strong>For principals:</strong> agreeing on rules for AI-use disclosure and process evidence, and not basing sanctions on detectors.</li>
<li><strong>For families:</strong> the message is not "writing no longer matters", but "writing with judgment is what cannot be delegated".</li>
</ul>

<h2>Colombia, Latin America and the world</h2>
<p>The evidence in this article comes mostly from English-language studies and global platforms; the Spanish-language market has particularities (an enormous amount of low-cost content, translation and marketing copy, high exposure of freelancers in the region) that deserve their own studies, and I do not yet know robust local figures on prices of human text versus AI text. One sign to watch is origin signals: marks like Human Authored currently apply to books in the United States. In Latin America and Colombia, differences in access to AI and to language may widen the gap between those who use the tool with judgment and those who depend on it. It is the same underlying discussion as <a href="/gran-mentira-ia-inteligencia-artificial-no-piensa/">the great lie about AI</a> and <a href="/las-matematicas-estan-a-punto-de-morir-openai-372-resultados-verificacion-lean/">is math dying?</a> (both in Spanish): when producing is cheap, what is scarce is understanding and answering for what is produced.</p>

