<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Is it true that AI texts will all be the same?</h3>
<p>"Default" texts tend to converge, according to an experiment with 293 writers; with specific instructions and human editing they can be very different. "All the same" is an exaggeration.</p>
<h3>Can we tell a human text from an AI one?</h3>
<p>In short texts and in a 2023 study, readers were right 50% to 52% of the time, nearly chance; automatic detectors are not reliable either.</p>
<h3>Will more be paid for human-written work?</h3>
<p>There are no data showing it; the available market studies show drops in employment and earnings in exposed occupations. A premium for the verifiably human with its own voice, in niches, is more plausible.</p>
<h3>What makes a text valuable in the age of AI?</h3>
<p>Accountable authorship, first-hand experience, selection judgment and the trust of a particular reader, more than the text's correctness.</p>
<h3>How do I use the script?</h3>
<p>Run <code>python medidor_homogeneidad.py</code> to see the example, or pass it a folder with one .txt per text to measure yours.</p>

<p class="notice"><strong>Check your own claims.</strong> Download the <a href="/descargas/textos-iguales/evaluacion-afirmacion-textos-humanos.xlsx">workbook</a> (in Spanish), change your confidences in each part and see how the conclusion changes; and measure your class's texts with the <a href="/descargas/textos-iguales/medidor_homogeneidad.py">script</a>.</p>

<h2>Food for thought</h2>
<p>A machine can write a correct text on any topic, but it cannot have been where you were or answer by name for what it says. <strong>What do you know, first-hand, that is not on any network or in any model? And if everything else became abundant, how would you make whoever reads you know that behind that text there is someone who lived it, thought it and answers for it?</strong></p>
